Welcome to Module 2. Over the next four weeks, we're going to take the fundamentals you've been building and put them to work on real projects. Each week, we'll build something together in class, and then for homework you'll build your own version from scratch.

We're starting with one of the most common and useful things news developers build: a database explorer. That's a searchable, filterable page that lets readers dig through records on their own. Newsrooms use them to put public data directly in the hands of their audience.

## Part 1: What is a database explorer?

You've probably used a database explorer without thinking about it. They're everywhere in journalism.

ProPublica's [Nonprofit Explorer](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/) lets you search the tax filings of every nonprofit in America. The Marshall Project's [The Next to Die](https://www.themarshallproject.org/next-to-die) tracks upcoming executions. WNYC built [a tool](https://projects.wnyc.org/rent-stabilized/) that lets you check whether your building is rent-stabilized. The New York Times publishes [a database of police misconduct records](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/10/20/us/nypd-misconduct-discipline-records.html).

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/propublica-nonprofits.png"
  alt="ProPublica's Nonprofit Explorer showing a search interface with a text input and results listing nonprofit organizations"
  chromeTitle="Nonprofit Explorer"
  chromeUrl="https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

What these tools have in common is a simple pattern: a list page where you can search and filter, and a detail page where you can drill into an individual record. That's what we're going to build today.

### Our dataset

We'll be working with housing violation data from the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development, known as HPD. Every time an HPD inspector visits a building and finds a problem, they issue a violation. These violations come in four classes:

- **Class A** — Non-hazardous. Minor issues like a missing doorbell.
- **Class B** — Hazardous. More serious problems like a broken lock or a leaky faucet.
- **Class C** — Immediately hazardous. The most dangerous conditions: no heat in winter, lead paint in a child's room, a rat infestation, a broken fire escape.
- **Class I** — Information orders. Administrative notices to building owners.

We're going to focus on Class C, the most dangerous category. Our dataset covers the Bronx, the borough with the highest concentration of housing violations in the city.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/hpd-open-data.png"
  alt="The HPD Housing Maintenance Code Violations dataset on the NYC Open Data portal, showing a table of violation records"
  chromeTitle="Housing Maintenance Code Violations"
  chromeUrl="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Housing-Development/Housing-Maintenance-Code-Violations/wvxf-dwi5"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The raw dataset on NYC Open Data contains millions of individual violation records going back to 2010. That's far too much data for a static site to handle. So I've prepared a smaller file for class that rolls up the violations at the building level. Instead of one row per violation, it's one row per building, with a count of how many open Class C violations each building has and the details nested inside.

The editorial question we're answering: **Which Bronx buildings have the most immediately hazardous housing violations right now?**

### Set up your project

By now, this part should feel like muscle memory.

Open your browser and navigate to our template repository at <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>. Click "Use this template" and create a new repository called `bronx-housing-violations`. Make sure "Public" is selected, then click "Create repository."

Clone the repository to your computer and open it in Visual Studio Code. Open a terminal and run:

```bash
npm install
```

Then start the development server:

```bash
npm run dev
```

Visit [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173) to make sure it's working. You should see the familiar template page.

## Part 2: Loading the data

I'm going to give you a file called `bronx-buildings.json`. Download it from the link I've posted in the class chat and save it in your project at `src/lib/data/bronx-buildings.json`.

You'll need to create the `data` folder inside `src/lib/` first. Right-click on `src/lib/` in the Explorer sidebar and select "New Folder." Name it `data`. Then move or save the JSON file there.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/vscode-data-folder.png"
  alt="VS Code Explorer showing the src/lib/data/ folder with bronx-buildings.json inside it"
  showChrome={false}
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Before we do anything with this data, let's look at it. Open `bronx-buildings.json` in VS Code and scroll through it. You'll see an array of objects, each representing a building. Here's what one looks like:

```json
{
  "id": "34291",
  "address": "1520 GRAND CONCOURSE",
  "zip": "10457",
  "violationCount": 47,
  "latestDate": "2026-02-14",
  "lat": 40.8441,
  "lng": -73.9106,
  "violations": [
    {
      "description": "SECTION 27-2005 HMC: REPAIR THE BROKEN OR DEFECTIVE PLASTERED SURFACES AND PAINT IN A UNIFORM COLOR HALLWAY, 3RD STORY",
      "date": "2026-02-14"
    },
    {
      "description": "SECTION 27-2005 HMC: ABATE THE NUISANCE CONSISTING OF VERMIN, ROACH CONDITION IN ENTIRE APARTMENT, 2ND STORY, APARTMENT 2B",
      "date": "2026-01-22"
    }
  ]
}
```

Each building has a handful of top-level fields — `id`, `address`, `zip`, `violationCount`, `latestDate`, `lat`, and `lng` — plus a `violations` array containing the individual violation records with their descriptions and dates.

The descriptions are a bit bureaucratic, but they paint a vivid picture of what tenants are living with. Broken plaster. Roaches. Lead paint. No heat. These are the conditions that HPD considers immediately hazardous to the people who live there.

Now let's load the data into our page. Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Clear out the existing template content so we're starting from a clean slate. Replace everything with:

```svelte
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-buildings.json';

  console.log(buildings.length, 'buildings loaded');
  console.log(buildings[0]);
</script>

<h1>Bronx Housing Violations</h1>
<p>{buildings.length} buildings with open Class C violations</p>
```

Save the file, then open your browser's developer console (right-click anywhere on the page, select "Inspect," and click the "Console" tab). You should see the number of buildings and the first record logged out.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/console-log.png"
  alt="Browser developer console showing the number of buildings loaded and the first record object expanded to show its fields"
  chromeTitle="Bronx Housing Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The `import` statement at the top is how SvelteKit loads a JSON file. The `$lib` path points to your `src/lib/` folder, so the full path resolves to the file you just saved. Once imported, `buildings` is a regular JavaScript array that you can loop through, filter, sort, and display.

Remove the `console.log` lines once you've confirmed the data is loading. They were just for testing.

## Part 3: Building the list

Now let's display the buildings on the page. We'll use the `{#each}` loop you learned in a previous class to iterate through the array and render a card for each building.

Add an `{#each}` block below the headline:

```svelte
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-buildings.json';
</script>

<h1>Bronx Housing Violations</h1>
<p>{buildings.length} buildings with open Class C violations</p>

{#each buildings as building}
  <div class="card">
    <h3>{building.address}</h3>
    <p>{building.zip} · {building.violationCount} violations</p>
  </div>
{/each}
```

Save and check your browser. You should see a long list of buildings with their addresses and violation counts.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/raw-list.png"
  alt="A long unstyled list of Bronx buildings showing addresses and violation counts"
  chromeTitle="Bronx Housing Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

It works, but the buildings are in whatever order the data file has them. Let's sort them so the buildings with the most violations appear first. We can do this by chaining JavaScript's [`.sort()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/sort) method onto the array right in the `{#each}` block. But a cleaner approach is to create a sorted copy in the script block:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="4"}
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-buildings.json';

  let sorted = buildings.sort((a, b) => b.violationCount - a.violationCount);
</script>
```

The `.sort()` method takes a comparison function. This one says: "compare two buildings by subtracting `a`'s count from `b`'s count." When the result is positive, `b` comes first. That gives us descending order — the worst buildings at the top.

Update your `{#each}` to loop over `sorted` instead of `buildings`:

```svelte
{#each sorted as building}
```

Save and check. The building with the most violations should now be at the top of the list.

### Extracting a BuildingCard component

Our card markup is going to get more complex as we add features. Let's pull it out into its own component now, while it's still simple.

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/BuildingCard.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { building } = $props();
</script>

<div class="card">
  <h3>{building.address}</h3>
  <div class="meta">
    <span class="zip">{building.zip}</span>
    <span class="count">{building.violationCount} violations</span>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .card {
    padding: 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-light-gray);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .card:hover {
    background-color: var(--color-light-gray);
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .meta {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-dark-gray);
  }

  .count {
    font-weight: bold;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
</style>
```

This component accepts a single `building` prop and renders the card. The styles are scoped to this component so they won't leak into the rest of the page.

Now update `+page.svelte` to use the new component:

```svelte
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-buildings.json';
  import BuildingCard from '$lib/components/BuildingCard.svelte';

  let sorted = buildings.sort((a, b) => b.violationCount - a.violationCount);
</script>

<h1>Bronx Housing Violations</h1>
<p>{sorted.length} buildings with open Class C violations</p>

{#each sorted as building}
  <BuildingCard {building} />
{/each}
```

Notice the shorthand `{building}` instead of `building={building}`. When the prop name and the variable name are the same, Svelte lets you skip the repetition. Save and verify it looks the same.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/building-cards.png"
  alt="A styled list of building cards showing addresses, zip codes, and violation counts, sorted with the highest counts first"
  chromeTitle="Bronx Housing Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

## Part 4: Search and filters

A list of hundreds of buildings isn't very useful without a way to narrow it down. Let's add a search box and a filter dropdown.

We'll use the same reactive tools you learned in [Week 4](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-4/): `$state` for tracking what the user has typed or selected, `$derived` for automatically recalculating the filtered list, and `bind:value` for connecting inputs to state variables.

### Adding a search input

Start by adding a `$state` variable for the search term and a `$derived` variable for the filtered results. Replace the `sorted` variable:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="4-11"}
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-buildings.json';
  import BuildingCard from '$lib/components/BuildingCard.svelte';

  let search = $state('');

  let filtered = $derived(
    buildings
      .filter(b => b.address.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase()))
      .sort((a, b) => b.violationCount - a.violationCount)
  );
</script>
```

The `$derived` expression does two things. First, it filters the buildings array to only include those whose address contains the search term. The [`.toLowerCase()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/toLowerCase) calls on both sides make the search case-insensitive, so typing "grand" will match "GRAND CONCOURSE." Second, it sorts the results by violation count, just as before.

Whenever `search` changes — because the user typed something — Svelte automatically re-runs the `$derived` expression and updates everything on the page that depends on it.

Now add a text input bound to the `search` variable, above the `{#each}` loop:

```svelte
<input
  type="text"
  placeholder="Search by address..."
  bind:value={search}
/>

<p>{filtered.length} buildings</p>

{#each filtered as building}
  <BuildingCard {building} />
{/each}
```

Save and try it. Type an address into the search box and watch the list narrow in real time.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/search-working.png"
  alt="The building list with a search input at the top. The user has typed 'grand' and the list shows only buildings on Grand Concourse."
  chromeTitle="Bronx Housing Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

### Adding a zip code filter

Now let's add a dropdown that filters by zip code. First, we need a list of all the unique zip codes in the dataset. We can build it with a JavaScript technique that extracts one value from every record and removes duplicates:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="5"}
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-buildings.json';
  import BuildingCard from '$lib/components/BuildingCard.svelte';

  let zips = [...new Set(buildings.map(b => b.zip))].sort();
  let search = $state('');
  let zipFilter = $state('all');

  let filtered = $derived(
    buildings
      .filter(b => {
        let matchesSearch = b.address.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase());
        let matchesZip = zipFilter === 'all' || b.zip === zipFilter;
        return matchesSearch && matchesZip;
      })
      .sort((a, b) => b.violationCount - a.violationCount)
  );
</script>
```

That first new line does a lot in a small space, so let's unpack it. Reading from the inside out:

- `buildings.map(b => b.zip)` creates a new array containing just the zip code from every building. If there are 500 buildings, this produces an array of 500 zip codes, many of them duplicates.
- `new Set(...)` converts that array into a [Set](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Set), which is a special JavaScript data structure that automatically removes duplicates.
- `[...` `]` spreads the Set back into a regular array, so we can call `.sort()` on it.
- `.sort()` puts the zip codes in order.

The result is a clean list of unique zip codes like `["10451", "10452", "10453", ...]` that we can use to populate a dropdown menu.

We also added a `zipFilter` state variable with a default value of `'all'`, and updated the `$derived` filter to check both conditions. A building must match the search text **and** the zip code filter to appear in the results.

Now add a `<select>` dropdown bound to `zipFilter`, next to the search input:

```svelte
<div class="controls">
  <input
    type="text"
    placeholder="Search by address..."
    bind:value={search}
  />

  <select bind:value={zipFilter}>
    <option value="all">All zip codes</option>
    {#each zips as zip}
      <option value={zip}>{zip}</option>
    {/each}
  </select>
</div>

<p>{filtered.length} buildings</p>
```

Notice that we're using `{#each}` inside the `<select>` to generate an `<option>` for every zip code. The first option, "All zip codes," has the value `'all'`, which matches the default value of `zipFilter`. When the user selects a specific zip, the filter kicks in.

Let's add some basic styles. Add a `<style>` block at the bottom of `+page.svelte`:

```css
.controls {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1rem;
  margin: 1rem 0;
}

input[type="text"] {
  flex: 1;
  padding: 0.5rem;
  font-size: 1rem;
  border: 2px solid var(--color-light-gray);
}

select {
  padding: 0.5rem;
  font-size: 1rem;
  border: 2px solid var(--color-light-gray);
}
```

Save and check your browser. You should now be able to search by address, filter by zip code, or combine both. The count above the list updates automatically as you change the filters.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/filters-working.png"
  alt="The building list with both a search input and a zip code dropdown. A zip code is selected and the count shows a smaller number of filtered results."
  chromeTitle="Bronx Housing Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Try selecting a zip code and then typing in the search box. The filters compose — they both apply at the same time. This is the same reactive pattern you used in Week 4, now applied to a real dataset with real consequences.

## Part 5: Detail pages

So far, everything lives on a single page. But a good database explorer lets you click into individual records and see their full story. That's what we'll build now.

In SvelteKit, every page on your site is a file inside the `src/routes/` folder. Up until now, we've only used `src/routes/+page.svelte`, which is the homepage. But SvelteKit can create pages at any URL by adding folders and files inside `src/routes/`.

We want a separate page for every building, at a URL like `/building/34291`, where the number is the building's ID. To do this, we'll use a SvelteKit feature called [dynamic routes](https://kit.svelte.dev/docs/routing#page).

### Creating the route

Create a new folder structure inside `src/routes/`:

```
src/routes/building/[id]/
```

The square brackets around `[id]` are the key. They tell SvelteKit that this part of the URL is a variable — it could be any value. When someone visits `/building/34291`, SvelteKit will load this route and set `id` to `"34291"`.

Inside that folder, create two files. The first is `+page.js`. This is a new type of file we haven't used before. It's called a [load function](https://kit.svelte.dev/docs/load), and its job is to prepare data before the page renders.

Create `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.js`:

```js
import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-buildings.json';

export function load({ params }) {
  const building = buildings.find(b => b.id === params.id);
  return { building };
}

export function entries() {
  return buildings.map(b => ({ id: b.id }));
}
```

There are two functions here. Let's take them one at a time.

The `load` function receives a `params` object containing the dynamic parts of the URL. Since our folder is called `[id]`, `params.id` will contain whatever value appears in the URL. We use JavaScript's [`.find()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/find) method to locate the matching building in our dataset, and return it as an object.

The `entries` function serves a different purpose. Because our site is a static site — all the pages are built ahead of time, not generated on-the-fly — SvelteKit needs to know every possible page that could exist. The `entries` function provides that list. It maps over all the buildings and returns an array of objects, each containing an `id` value. When you build the site, SvelteKit will loop through these and generate a separate HTML page for each one.

### Creating the detail page

Now create the page that will display the building's details. Create `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { data } = $props();
  let building = data.building;
</script>

<a href="/">&larr; Back to all buildings</a>

<h1>{building.address}</h1>

<div class="summary">
  <p><strong>{building.violationCount}</strong> open Class C violations</p>
  <p>ZIP code: {building.zip}</p>
  <p>Most recent inspection: {building.latestDate}</p>
</div>

<h2>Violations</h2>

{#each building.violations as violation}
  <div class="violation">
    <p class="description">{violation.description}</p>
    <p class="date">{violation.date}</p>
  </div>
{/each}
```

The key line is the first one in the script block: `let { data } = $props()`. When a page has a `+page.js` load function, SvelteKit passes whatever it returns as a `data` prop. So `data.building` is the building object we found in the load function.

The rest is familiar. We display the building's address as a headline, show a summary of its violation count and dates, and then loop through the `violations` array to list each one.

Add some styles at the bottom:

```css
.summary {
  padding: 1rem;
  background: var(--color-light-gray);
  margin: 1rem 0;
}

.summary p {
  margin: 0.25rem 0;
}

.violation {
  padding: 1rem 0;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-light-gray);
}

.description {
  margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
  line-height: 1.4;
}

.date {
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  color: var(--color-dark-gray);
  margin: 0;
}
```

Save the file. We can't navigate to a detail page yet because we haven't linked to it. Let's fix that.

### Linking to detail pages

Open `src/lib/components/BuildingCard.svelte`. We need to wrap the card in a link that points to the building's detail page.

At the top of the file, add an import for the base path:

```js
import { base } from '$app/paths';
```

The `base` variable is a SvelteKit helper that accounts for the fact that your site might not live at the root of a domain. On GitHub Pages, your site is at a URL like `yourusername.github.io/bronx-housing-violations/`, not just `yourusername.github.io/`. The `base` variable contains that `/bronx-housing-violations` prefix so your links work correctly.

Now wrap the card content in an `<a>` tag:

```svelte
<script>
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  let { building } = $props();
</script>

<a class="card" href="{base}/building/{building.id}">
  <h3>{building.address}</h3>
  <div class="meta">
    <span class="zip">{building.zip}</span>
    <span class="count">{building.violationCount} violations</span>
  </div>
</a>
```

We replaced the `<div>` with an `<a>` tag and set its `href` to the detail page URL. The URL is built from three pieces: the base path, the `/building/` prefix, and the building's ID.

Update the styles. Add these rules to make the link behave like a card instead of a traditional blue underlined link:

```css
a.card {
  display: block;
  text-decoration: none;
  color: inherit;
}
```

Save and click on a building in the list. You should land on a detail page showing that building's address and all of its violations.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/detail-page.png"
  alt="A detail page for a specific Bronx building showing the address as a headline, a summary box with violation count, zip code, and latest inspection date, followed by a list of individual violations with descriptions and dates"
  chromeTitle="1520 GRAND CONCOURSE"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173/building/34291"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Click the "Back to all buildings" link at the top to return to the list. Your search and filter state will reset — that's expected for a static site. Each page load starts fresh.

Also update the back link on the detail page to use the `base` path. In `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.svelte`, add the import and update the link:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="3,7"}
<script>
  let { data } = $props();
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  let building = data.building;
</script>

<a href="{base}/">&larr; Back to all buildings</a>
```

## Part 6: Adding a locator map

Each building in our dataset has latitude and longitude coordinates. Let's use them to add a small map to the detail page that shows where the building is located.

We'll create a simple component that embeds an [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/) map centered on the building. This doesn't require installing any new packages — it uses an iframe, which is just a way of embedding one web page inside another.

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/LocatorMap.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { lat, lng } = $props();

  let src = $derived(
    `https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=${lng - 0.005},${lat - 0.005},${lng + 0.005},${lat + 0.005}&layer=mapnik&marker=${lat},${lng}`
  );
</script>

<div class="map-container">
  <iframe
    title="Building location"
    width="100%"
    height="300"
    {src}
    frameborder="0"
  ></iframe>
  <p class="map-credit">
    Map data © <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/" target="_blank">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors
  </p>
</div>

<style>
  .map-container {
    margin: 1rem 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-light-gray);
  }

  iframe {
    display: block;
  }

  .map-credit {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-dark-gray);
    text-align: right;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.5rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
```

The component accepts `lat` and `lng` as props. It constructs a URL for OpenStreetMap's embed service, which takes a bounding box (the area to show) and a marker position. The `$derived` expression builds this URL dynamically. We subtract and add 0.005 degrees from the coordinates to create a small box around the building — that's roughly a couple of blocks in each direction.

Now add the map to the detail page. Open `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.svelte` and import the component:

```js
import LocatorMap from '$lib/components/LocatorMap.svelte';
```

Place it in the page, after the summary and before the violations list:

```svelte
<LocatorMap lat={building.lat} lng={building.lng} />
```

Save and visit a building's detail page. You should see a small interactive map showing the building's location in the Bronx.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/locator-map.png"
  alt="The detail page now includes a small embedded OpenStreetMap showing a red marker at the building's location, with the surrounding Bronx streets visible"
  chromeTitle="1520 GRAND CONCOURSE"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173/building/34291"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

In a couple of weeks, we'll learn how to build full interactive maps from scratch using a library called MapLibre. For now, this simple embed is enough to orient the reader.

## Part 7: Editorial polish

The data is loaded, the search works, and the detail pages are in place. But right now the page is just a database. It needs a frame — a headline, some context, and a source credit — to make it a piece of journalism.

Go back to `src/routes/+page.svelte` and update the top of the page with an editorial introduction:

```svelte
<h1>Bronx Buildings with Hazardous Housing Violations</h1>

<p class="intro">
  New York City's Department of Housing Preservation and Development inspects
  residential buildings and issues violations when it finds conditions that
  violate the housing maintenance code. Class C violations are the most
  serious — they describe conditions the city considers immediately
  hazardous to tenants' health and safety, including lead paint, no heat
  in winter, and pest infestations.
</p>

<p class="intro">
  The buildings listed below are in the Bronx, the borough with the highest
  concentration of housing violations in the city. They are ranked by the
  number of open Class C violations on record.
</p>
```

At the bottom of the page, after the `{#each}` loop, add a source credit:

```svelte
<footer class="source">
  <p>
    Source: <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Housing-Development/Housing-Maintenance-Code-Violations/wvxf-dwi5" target="_blank">NYC Department of Housing Preservation and Development</a>,
    via NYC Open Data. Class C (immediately hazardous) violations for
    buildings in the Bronx. Data as of March 2026.
  </p>
  <p>
    For more information about your building's violations, visit
    <a href="https://hpdonline.nyc.gov/hpdonline/provide-information" target="_blank">HPD Online</a>.
  </p>
</footer>
```

Add styles for the intro and footer:

```css
.intro {
  font-size: 1.0625rem;
  line-height: 1.6;
  max-width: 640px;
}

.source {
  margin-top: 2rem;
  padding-top: 1rem;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--color-light-gray);
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  color: var(--color-dark-gray);
}
```

