  src="/screenshots/week-7/final-page.png"
  alt="The finished database explorer showing a headline, two paragraphs of editorial context, the search and filter controls, the list of building cards, and a source credit at the bottom"
  chromeTitle="Bronx Buildings with Hazardous Housing Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Save, commit, and push to GitHub. Watch the Actions tab to make sure the deploy succeeds, then check your live site.

Take a moment to browse through the data. Click into the buildings at the top of the list and read the violation descriptions. What patterns do you notice? What stories does this data tell? That instinct — the impulse to look at the records and ask questions — is what separates a database explorer from just a spreadsheet.

## Homework

### Task 1: Build your own database explorer

Find a dataset on the [NYC Open Data portal](https://opendata.cityofnewyork.us/) or another public data source with enough detail per record to support a searchable list page and detail pages. Build a database explorer for it.

Your project must have:

* A searchable or filterable list page with at least two controls (search input, dropdown, toggle — your choice)
* Detail pages for individual records or groups of records
* An editorial frame: a headline, introductory text, and a source credit

Some datasets that could work well:

* FDNY inspections by building
* NYC school quality reports and ratings
* Licensed food vendors
* Dog licensing records
* NYPD arrest records by precinct

But you are not limited to NYC. If you find a compelling public dataset from another source, go for it. The point is to find data that has a story in it and build a tool that lets readers find it.

Deploy it to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to share your database explorer with the class and explain how you built it. You should be prepared to:

* Show the dataset you chose and explain why it's interesting
* Walk through your search and filter logic
* Show a detail page and explain how the route works
* Discuss what editorial choices you made — what did you include, what did you leave out, and why
